ref('polyakov1981quantum','Polyakov, Alexander M.','Quantum geometry of bosonic strings',1981,'https://qft.itp.ac.ru/polyakov-2.pdf',journal='Physics Letters B',volume=103,number=3,pages='207--210',doi='10.1016/0370-2693(81)90743-7',annote='Full four-page original opened and read, equations3--31. Received26May1981, published23July1981. Formal path-integral construction and anomaly calculation, not a rigorous all-dimension random-surface measure theorem.')
cumulative('kac1949distributions','JSTOR publisher issue contents actually opened at https://www.jstor.org/stable/i308291, confirming January1949, volume65(1),pp1--13. Original AMS PDF blocked. Only metadata verified; heat-kernel and resolvent computations in this shard are direct editorial derivations, not quoted as an original theorem.')
bib['kac1949distributions']=re.sub(r'url\s*=\s*\{[^}]+\}',r'url = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/i308291}',bib['kac1949distributions'])
talk(567,'1982-09-27','A. B. Zamolodchikov','P001041-P001047','From random paths to Polyakov sums over surfaces',r'The entry begins on P001043 and ends above the 4 October heading on P001046. Classical area and conformal coordinates lead into three path regularizations: lattice walks, fixed-length random rods, and Gaussian increments. Their properly normalized diffusive limits motivate the intrinsic-metric integral for surfaces. Several formulas omit scaling factors, and the passage from fixed-metric Gaussian fields to a sum over geometries remains a formal construction.')
add(567,'Area, conformal coordinates and the minimal-surface equation','P001043','construction',r'\(G_{ab}=\partial_aX\cdot\partial_bX\), \(A=\int\sqrt{\det G}\); in a gauge \(G_{ab}=\rho\delta_{ab}\), the equations are \(\partial_a\partial_aX=0\), \(\partial_1X\cdot\partial_2X=0\), and \(|\partial_1X|^2=|\partial_2X|^2\).',r'For a smooth immersed Euclidean surface, local isothermal coordinates make the induced metric conformally flat. In these coordinates the mean-curvature-zero equation is harmonicity of \(X\), together with conformality. Branch points and global conformal moduli require additional treatment. A stationary minimal immersion need not be a global area minimizer.',r'The same classical formulation is recalled in \citet[Equations~(3)--(11)]{polyakov1981quantum}, published July 1981.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Locality, immersion and the distinction between stationary and globally minimizing surfaces are explicit.')
add(567,'Propagation of conformality from the boundary','P001043','claim',r'The two conformality conditions need only hold on the boundary.',r'Let \(X:\overline\Omega\to\mathbb R^d\) be \(C^1\) on a bounded smooth planar closure and harmonic in \(\Omega\). Its Hopf function \(H=X_z\cdot X_z\) is holomorphic. If both conformality equations hold on every boundary component, then \(H=0\) on the boundary, hence throughout \(\Omega\) by the maximum principle. Where \(dX\ne0\), \(X\) is a minimal immersion.',r'This elementary implication belongs to the classical conformal formulation discussed in \citet[Equations~(5)--(11)]{polyakov1981quantum}. The proof here \\ours supplies the boundary regularity and maximum-principle argument absent from the notes.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The bounded planar domain, regularity, harmonicity and nonbranch-point qualification are added.')
add(567,'What measure defines the sum over surfaces?','P001043','question',r'\(\sum_{\text{surfaces}}e^{-\mu_0^2A}\), followed by \ru{Мера --- ?}',r'The expression needs an ensemble, topology, embedding or mapping convention, parametrization quotient, boundary data, cutoff and normalization. The bare area weight alone does not determine a measure on a space of surfaces.',r'\citet[Equation~(14)]{polyakov1981quantum} proposes an intrinsic-metric formalism. It does not identify a unique measure for every interpretation of the notebook sum.',('polyakov1981quantum',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='read the result',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='No unspecified lattice-surface or Liouville existence problem is silently substituted.',status='the general measure question is underdetermined; precise subclasses must be assessed separately.')
add(567,'Reparametrization invariance of curve length','P001043','construction',r'Length \(L=\int|\dot X|\,dt\) is invariant under changes of parameter; minimizing gives \(\ddot X=0\), with \(|\dot X|=1\).',r'For a regular curve with fixed endpoints, the Euler--Lagrange equation is \(d(\dot X/|\dot X|)/dt=0\). It becomes \(\ddot X=0\) after choosing arclength, with constant unit tangent. The interval length must equal the endpoint distance for a unit-speed minimizing segment.',r'This is the elementary point-particle analogue used in \citet[Equation~(1)]{polyakov1981quantum}; the precise gauge-fixed equation follows by direct variation.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The order of variation and gauge choice, regularity and endpoint compatibility are supplied.')
add(567,'The lattice-walk geometric series and its convergence range','P001044','method',r'Replace \(\mathbb R^d\) by a lattice, give every step length one, and sum \(e^{-mN}\); the notes say the sum converges and is explicitly computable.',r'For unrestricted nearest-neighbor walks, the Fourier multiplier for the unnormalized step count is \(A(p)=2\sum_{j=1}^d\cos p_j\). The Green series is \((1-e^{-m}A(p))^{-1}\) when the Neumann series converges, in particular for \(2de^{-m}<1\). At the critical weight convergence of a fixed-endpoint Green function depends on dimension; below critical mass the unqualified convergence assertion fails.',r'The path-sum framework predates the seminar; compare \citet{polyakov1981quantum}. The finite-step multiplier, norm bound and geometric series are calculated directly here.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The walk model, normalization and sufficient uniform convergence condition are specified; no necessity claim is made at a single endpoint.')
add(567,'Fixed-length random rods and their endpoint distribution','P001044','construction',r'\(G_N(R)=\int\prod d n_i\,\delta(\sum n_i-R/\Delta)\); Fourier transformation gives \(C(p,\Delta)^N\), and a recurrence is requested.',r'With normalized rotation-invariant measure \(\sigma\) on \(S^{d-1}\), the endpoint density is \(p_N(R)=\int\delta(R-\Delta\sum n_i)\prod d\sigma(n_i)=\Delta^{-d}G_N(R)\). It satisfies \(p_{N+1}(R)=\int p_N(R-\Delta n)d\sigma(n)\) and \(\widehat p_N=C^N\), where \(C=\int e^{i\Delta p\cdot n}d\sigma(n)\). For \(d\ge2\), \(C=\Gamma(d/2)(2/(\Delta|p|))^{d/2-1}J_{d/2-1}(\Delta|p|)\), continuously extended at zero.',r'This is a direct finite-dimensional derivation of the notebook regularization. Its historical role is the path analogue of \citet[Equations~(1)--(2)]{polyakov1981quantum}.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='Probability normalization, the missing delta-function Jacobian and the recurrence are supplied.')
add(567,'Diffusive scaling, mass tuning and the quadratic momentum term','P001044-P001045','method',r'Equations (19)--(20) sum the step characteristic function and then use an exponent \(a+b\vec p-m\), before obtaining a propagator \(1/(p^2+m_R^2)\).',r'For isotropic unit steps, \(C(p,\Delta)=1-\Delta^2|p|^2/(2d)+O(\Delta^4|p|^4)\). Put \(a_\Delta=\Delta^2/(2d)\) and choose per-step weight \(q_\Delta=1-m_R^2a_\Delta+o(a_\Delta)\). Then \(a_\Delta\sum_{N\ge0}(q_\Delta C)^N\to(m_R^2+|p|^2)^{-1}\) for \(m_R>0\). Unnormalized step measures require additional entropy compensation in \(q_\Delta\).',r'The limit follows by expansion and a geometric series, within the path framework of \citet{polyakov1981quantum}. A linear isotropic momentum term cannot produce it.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='A definite scaling limit replaces the undefined constants a,b and clarifies mass renormalization.')
add(567,'Gaussian increments, Wiener measure and proper time','P001045','method',r'The rods become springs, their lengths are called Gaussian, and a formal integral \(e^{-\frac12\int\dot X^2}\) over paths is called Wiener measure.',r'Gaussian displacement vectors with covariance \(\Delta I\), each normalized by \((2\pi\Delta)^{-d/2}\), converge to Brownian motion with generator \(\tfrac12\Delta_x\). The endpoint heat kernel has Fourier multiplier \(e^{-\tau|p|^2/2}\). Integrating against \(e^{-a\tau}\,d\tau\), \(a>0\), yields \((a+|p|^2/2)^{-1}\). Brownian paths have infinite ordinary arclength almost surely; the kinetic action is symbolic rather than a finite pathwise function.',r'The Wiener-functional tradition precedes the seminar; \citet{kac1949distributions} is verified bibliographically only. The Gaussian convolution and Laplace transform above are checked directly. Matching the previous convention requires rescaling time and mass.',('kac1949distributions',),literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='Displacements replace Gaussian lengths; normalization, generator and the symbolic action convention are explicit.')
add(567,'The Euclidean massive Green function','P001045','claim',r'\(\widehat G(p)=z/(p^2+m^2)\), whose inverse is called a Hankel function.',r'With Fourier inverse \((2\pi)^{-d}\int e^{ip\cdot R}\,dp\), for \(m>0\), \(R\ne0\), the kernel is \(G(R)=(2\pi)^{-d/2}(m/|R|)^{d/2-1}K_{d/2-1}(m|R|)\). A Hankel-function expression requires analytic continuation to an imaginary argument or a Lorentzian prescription; the decaying Euclidean expression uses modified Bessel \(K\).',r'This follows by integrating the Gaussian heat kernel in proper time, the framework associated with \citet{kac1949distributions}. The normalization and function type are an editorial calculation.',('kac1949distributions',),verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The transform convention, massive regime and relation to the Hankel terminology are stated.')
add(567,'A spring mesh gives a Gaussian field at fixed worldsheet geometry','P001045','construction',r'A square spring network gives \(\int DX\exp[-\tfrac12\int(\partial X)^2]\); a regular grid is said to be insufficient for general surfaces.',r'For a fixed two-dimensional mesh and boundary conditions, a quadratic spring energy defines a finite-dimensional Gaussian measure after removing or fixing zero modes. Its continuum limit is a Gaussian free field at the chosen conformal structure. Varying embedded vertices on a fixed mesh does not by itself specify a sum over intrinsic metrics or topologies.',r'The distinction motivates \citet[Equations~(6), (14)]{polyakov1981quantum}. The notebook drawings do not define a unique discretization-independent surface measure.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='Boundary/zero-mode conditions and fixed intrinsic geometry are added.')
add(567,'Why the bosonic critical dimension is 26','P001045','claim',r'The fixed-metric quadratic action gives the \ru{прав. ответ} in target dimension 26.',r'In bosonic Polyakov gauge fixing, \(D\) free scalar matter fields contribute central charge \(D\), and the reparametrization ghosts contribute \(-26\). The local Weyl anomaly cancels at \(D=26\). This does not prove convergence over topologies, absence of tachyons, or existence of an unrestricted surface probability measure.',r'The coefficient is calculated in \citet[Equations~(23)--(25)]{polyakov1981quantum}, before the talk.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The anomaly statement replaces the unqualified phrase correct answer.')
add(567,'The independent-metric action and cosmological term','P001045-P001046','construction',r'The final integral introduces \(g_{ab}\) and \(+\lambda\int\sqrt g\) in the exponent, then says minimizing recovers the earlier equations.',r'The classical action \(S_P=\tfrac12\int\sqrt g\,g^{ab}\partial_aX\cdot\partial_bX\), varied in \(X\) and \(g\), imposes harmonicity and vanishing stress tensor. For a nondegenerate induced metric this makes \(g\) conformal to it and recovers area. A nonzero classical cosmological term changes the trace equation: in two dimensions the kinetic stress tensor is traceless, so that term must vanish. In the formal quantum measure it is instead a cutoff-dependent counterterm.',r'\citet[Equations~(6)--(7), (14), p.~209]{polyakov1981quantum} separates these roles. Gauge volume, moduli and boundary conditions also need specification.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The trace check explicitly separates the classical and quantum roles of lambda.')
corr('P001044','Unqualified lattice-walk convergence assertion',r'The sum over all walks with weight \(e^{-mN}\) converges.',r'Specify step normalization and mass range; \(2de^{-m}<1\) gives the uniform geometric-series regime for unnormalized nearest-neighbor walks.','Russian1044 read. There are (2d)^N unrestricted N-step walks; fixed-endpoint critical behavior has a separate dimensional qualification.')
corr('P001044','Endpoint identity and equations (15)--(18)',r'\(\sum n_i=(x_1-x_2)/\Delta\); Fourier delta integral has no Jacobian.',r'\(\sum n_i=(x_N-x_0)/\Delta\); use density \(\Delta^{-d}G_N\), or retain factor \(\Delta^d\) in its Fourier transform.','Russian1044 read; endpoint notation conflicts with the immediately defined R. Change of variables in the delta function gives the Jacobian.')
corr('P001044-P001045','Small-momentum expansion and equation (20)',r'Linear isotropic term \(b\vec p\) in the exponent.',r'First nonzero term \(-\Delta^2|p|^2/(2d)\) for normalized isotropic steps; tune step fugacity on the same scale.','Scan1045 and Russian1044 checked. Odd sphere moments vanish and covariance is I/d.')
corr('P001045','Gaussian spring paths, equations (22)--(23)',r'Gaussian lengths, unnormalized increments and immediate identification with the preceding proper-time convention.',r'Gaussian displacement vectors with normalized kernels; lengths have a chi-type distribution. Kinetic coefficient1/2 gives generator |p|^2/2, requiring time/mass rescaling to match (21).','Scan1045 inspected. Direct Gaussian convolution gives the factors; Brownian kinetic action is symbolic.')
corr('P001045','Function name after equation (24)',r'Inverse Euclidean propagator called a Hankel function.',r'Use modified Bessel K at positive real mass/distance, or state imaginary-argument analytic continuation.','Scan1045 inspected. The heat-kernel integral decays exponentially, unlike a real-argument oscillatory Hankel kernel.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001046','Classical minimization after equation (28)',r'Arbitrary nonzero cosmological term reproduces the original minimal-surface equations.',r'Classical equivalence uses zero cosmological term; the quantum term is a renormalization counterterm.','Russian1046 and Polyakov1981 pp208--209 read. The two-dimensional metric equation trace isolates lambda.')
audit.append('## Record 567\nBoundary1043--upper1046. Russian fully read; scan1045 inspected for p versus p squared, spring kernel, Hankel term and mesh drawings. Independent checks: Hopf differential boundary propagation, sphere characteristic, Jacobian, recurrence, isotropic covariance, diffusive resolvent and metric trace. Opened full Polyakov1981 original at https://qft.itp.ac.ru/polyakov-2.pdf, all4pages equations3--31; received26May1981, published23July1981. Formal integral claims are not treated as a rigorous universal surface measure theorem. Kac1949 AMS PDF blocked; JSTOR issue contents opened, metadata only verified, cumulative key reused. Brownian calculations are elementary editorial reconstructions. Earlier1980-495.7 is a thematic Feynman--Kac recurrence, not an exact surface-measure problem.')
